# The measured selection rule: MAXSCORE at three terms or fewer, WAND at
# four or more, at any k.
#
# First recalibrated from postings_decoded and docs_evaluated over a
# deterministic Zipf corpus at 2,000 and 100,000 documents, term counts
# two through six, k of 10 and 100 — replacing "WAND when terms <= 4 and
# k <= 10", which came from GOV2 on x86 at 25 million documents. That
# calibration put the boundary at three terms.
#
# Re-derived when MAXSCORE's probe order was fixed (descending by bound,
# metadata-only block bound before every seek): MAXSCORE became uniformly
# cheaper and took the three-term cell at both corpus sizes and both k,
# moving the boundary to four. The 2,000-document corpus at k=100 now
# prefers MAXSCORE everywhere, but both strategies cost about 2,000 units
# there; the rule follows the large-corpus cells, where every one agrees.
#
# Both strategies return identical results, so this is a cost decision
# with no quality risk. strategy_rule_matches_the_counters_it_was_
# calibrated_from re-derives it on every run.
#
# Owed: confirmation on BEIR. This corpus is synthetic.
min_terms_for_wand = 4
